capability set/empty  a set that holds nothing
  heroes  built-in {}
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::new, HashSet::default, BTreeSet::new, BTreeSet::default
  python  set, frozenset
  note    the empty map literal with its annotation, `s: {str: bool} = {}`; Go has no set, and its programs write `map[K]bool` or `map[K]struct{}`, which is this package's own shape

capability set/from-list  a set of the elements of a list
  heroes  sets.from_list
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::from, HashSet::from_iter, BTreeSet::from, BTreeSet::from_iter
  python  set, frozenset
  note    the elements stand in the order they first occur, where Python's set answers the order of its hash table, Rust's HashSet none and BTreeSet the sorted one; the order never counts for `==` or any question

capability set/contains  whether a set holds a value
  heroes  sets.contains
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::contains, BTreeSet::contains
  python  set.__contains__
  note    `s[x].default(false)` asks the same; a key under `false` is no element, so a removal written by hand as `s[x] @ false` is read as one

capability set/size  how many elements a set holds, and whether it holds none
  heroes  built-in len
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::len, HashSet::is_empty, BTreeSet::len, BTreeSet::is_empty
  python  set.__len__
  note    `s.len()` counts keys, so it is the size where every value is `true`, as in every set this package answers; `sets.to_list(s).len()` counts the elements of a map built by hand with keys under `false`

capability set/equal  whether two sets hold the same elements
  heroes  built-in ==
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::eq, HashSet::ne, BTreeSet::eq, BTreeSet::ne
  python  set.__eq__, set.__ne__
  note    the map's own `==`, on which insertion order does not count (observed: a set built in two orders is one key of a map); it compares the values too, so it answers equality of sets where every value is `true`

capability set/copy  a second set that changes apart from the first
  heroes  built-in =
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::clone, HashSet::clone_from, BTreeSet::clone, BTreeSet::clone_from
  python  set.copy, frozenset.copy
  note    every value is a copy, so `t = s` is one

capability set/immutable  a set nothing may change, usable as the key of a map or an element of a set
  heroes  built-in =
  go      -
  rust    BTreeSet::hash, BTreeSet::hash_slice
  python  frozenset, frozenset.__hash__
  note    a binding made with `=` never changes, and a `{K: bool}` is a valid key: observed, `{a, b}` and `{b, a}` are one key of a `{{str: bool}: i64}`, as two frozensets of one content are one key in Python

capability set/walk  every element of a set, in an order
  heroes  sets.to_list
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::iter, HashSet::into_iter, BTreeSet::iter, BTreeSet::into_iter
  python  set.__iter__
  note    the order the elements were first added; `for x in sets.to_list(s)` walks them. Rust's BTreeSet walks in sorted order, which is `sort(sets.to_list(s))` for numbers, texts and booleans and `lists.sort_by` for any other type

capability set/add  a set with one element more
  heroes  sets.add
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::insert, BTreeSet::insert, HashSet::get_or_insert, BTreeSet::get_or_insert
  python  set.add
  note    a new set, the one given unchanged; an element held already keeps its place. Rust's `insert` answers whether it was new, which is `!sets.contains(s, x)` asked before

capability set/add-many  a set with every element of a list added
  heroes  sets.add_all
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::extend, BTreeSet::extend
  python  set.update, set.__ior__
  note    one pass, where `add` in a loop copies the set at every call; Python's `update` takes several iterables, which is `add_all` with their elements concatenated or `sets.union_all`

capability set/remove  a set without one element
  heroes  sets.remove
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::remove, HashSet::take, BTreeSet::remove, BTreeSet::take
  python  set.remove, set.discard
  note    an element the set does not hold is no failure, as Python's `discard` and Rust's `remove`, where Python's `remove` raises KeyError: the caller who must know asks `sets.contains` first. Rust's `take` answers the element removed, which is the value the caller gave

capability set/remove-many  a set without the elements of a list
  heroes  sets.remove_all
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  set.difference_update, set.__isub__
  note    one pass over the list and one over the set; the elements of another set go with `sets.difference`

capability set/clear  a set emptied
  heroes  built-in {}
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::clear, HashSet::drain, BTreeSet::clear
  python  set.clear
  note    `s @ {}` on a cell; Rust's `drain` answers the elements as it empties, which is `sets.to_list(s)` read before

capability set/keep-where  the elements a function accepts
  heroes  sets.keep_where
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::retain, BTreeSet::retain
  python  -
  note    Python writes a set comprehension, `{x for x in s if f(x)}`; the function is called once for each element, in their order

capability set/remove-where  a set without the elements a function accepts
  heroes  sets.remove_where
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::extract_if, BTreeSet::extract_if
  python  -
  note    Rust's `extract_if` also answers the elements it took out, which is `sets.keep_where` with the same function

capability set/union  the elements either of two sets holds
  heroes  sets.union_of
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::union, HashSet::bitor, BTreeSet::union, BTreeSet::bitor, BTreeSet::append
  python  set.union, set.__or__, set.__ror__, frozenset.union
  note    named `union_of` because the compiler refuses `union` as a name, a keyword of C; the elements of the first stand first, then those only the second holds

capability set/union-many  the elements any of several sets holds
  heroes  sets.union_all
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  set.union, set.update
  note    Python's `union` takes any number of others; here the first set and a list of the others

capability set/intersection  the elements two sets both hold
  heroes  sets.intersection
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::intersection, HashSet::bitand, BTreeSet::intersection, BTreeSet::bitand
  python  set.intersection, set.__and__, set.__rand__, set.intersection_update, set.__iand__, frozenset.intersection
  note    in the order of the first set; `_update` writes the answer into the caller's own cell, `s @ sets.intersection(s: s, other: t)`

capability set/intersection-many  the elements all of several sets hold
  heroes  sets.intersection_all
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  set.intersection, set.intersection_update
  note    the first set and a list of the others, so the intersection of no set at all, which has no answer, cannot be asked; an empty list answers the first set, as Python's `s.intersection()` does

