capability set/take-one  an element taken out, with the set that is left
  heroes  refused: two answers of a type parameter are a record with a type parameter, and generics are on functions only
  go      -
  rust    BTreeSet::pop_first, BTreeSet::pop_last, BTreeSet::first, BTreeSet::last
  python  set.pop
  note    the first in the order of addition is `sets.to_list(s)[0]` where the set is not empty, and `sets.remove` takes it out; the smallest is the first of `sort(sets.to_list(s))`. Python's `pop` takes an element it does not name, which no program can count on

capability set/range  the elements between two bounds of an order
  heroes  sets.keep_where
  go      -
  rust    BTreeSet::range, BTreeSet::split_off, BTreeSet::lower_bound, BTreeSet::upper_bound, BTreeSet::lower_bound_mut, BTreeSet::upper_bound_mut
  python  -
  note    a set here is not kept sorted, so the elements within bounds are those a function of the bounds accepts, one pass; Rust's cursors walk a tree this package does not hold

dropped capacity  room asked of the allocator ahead of the elements: the language's map grows by itself and has no capacity to ask
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::with_capacity, HashSet::capacity, HashSet::reserve, HashSet::try_reserve, HashSet::shrink_to, HashSet::shrink_to_fit, HashSet::extend_reserve, HashSet::extend_one, BTreeSet::extend_reserve, BTreeSet::extend_one
  python  -

dropped hashers-and-allocators  the language's map has one hash and one allocator, neither of them a value
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::with_hasher, HashSet::with_capacity_and_hasher, HashSet::hasher, HashSet::new_in, HashSet::with_capacity_in, HashSet::with_hasher_in, HashSet::with_capacity_and_hasher_in, BTreeSet::new_in
  python  -

dropped borrows  what exists to reach the element a set stores rather than the one asked: every value is a copy and equality is structural, so the element held is the element asked
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::get, HashSet::replace, HashSet::get_or_insert_with, HashSet::entry, BTreeSet::get, BTreeSet::replace, BTreeSet::get_or_insert_with, BTreeSet::entry, hash_set::Entry, hash_set::OccupiedEntry, hash_set::VacantEntry, btree_set::Entry, btree_set::OccupiedEntry, btree_set::VacantEntry
  python  -

dropped iterator-and-cursor-types  a walk is a `for` over `sets.to_list(s)`, and a failure is a code and a message, so neither has a type of its own
  go      -
  rust    hash_set::Iter, hash_set::IntoIter, hash_set::Drain, hash_set::ExtractIf, hash_set::Difference, hash_set::Intersection, hash_set::SymmetricDifference, hash_set::Union, btree_set::Iter, btree_set::IntoIter, btree_set::Range, btree_set::ExtractIf, btree_set::Difference, btree_set::Intersection, btree_set::SymmetricDifference, btree_set::Union, btree_set::Cursor, btree_set::CursorMut, btree_set::CursorMutKey, btree_set::UnorderedKeyError
  python  -

dropped traits  what an interface would have needed: a set written as text for a debugger, ordered against another set by its sorted elements, converted, sized in memory
  go      -
  rust    HashSet::fmt, BTreeSet::fmt, BTreeSet::cmp, BTreeSet::partial_cmp, BTreeSet::lt, BTreeSet::le, BTreeSet::gt, BTreeSet::ge, BTreeSet::max, BTreeSet::min, BTreeSet::clamp, BTreeSet::clamp_to, and on both `type_id`, `borrow`, `borrow_mut`, `clone_to_uninit`, `into`, `to_owned`, `clone_into`, `try_from`, `try_into`
  python  set.__class_getitem__, set.__repr__, set.__str__, set.__format__, set.__sizeof__, set.__reduce__, set.__reduce_ex__, set.__getstate__, set.__init__, set.__new__, set.__hash__, and the object protocol every class has (`__class__`, `__delattr__`, `__dir__`, `__doc__`, `__getattribute__`, `__init_subclass__`, `__setattr__`, `__subclasshook__`)
